1
00:00:02,415 --> 00:00:06,807
This neural network is ninety-five percent sure this photo is a banana.

2
00:00:07,087 --> 00:00:08,959
Nice.

3
00:00:09,239 --> 00:00:13,247
But here is the question that bugged me: where did it look to decide that?

4
00:00:13,527 --> 00:00:15,399
Was it the banana?

5
00:00:15,679 --> 00:00:17,551
The background?

6
00:00:17,831 --> 00:00:19,703
The table?

7
00:00:19,983 --> 00:00:27,951
In the next ten minutes I will open the box, and you will see, pixel by pixel, what a real image classifier pays attention to.

8
00:00:28,231 --> 00:00:32,431
And I will show you the exact twenty lines of code that make it possible.

9
00:00:32,711 --> 00:00:39,431
I'm going to be upfront: I built this with Claude Code as my pair programmer, and the narration you hear is AI-generated.

10
00:00:39,711 --> 00:00:41,799
Everything you see running is real.

11
00:00:42,599 --> 00:00:48,239
Image models are everywhere: phone cameras, medical scans, self-driving cars.

12
00:00:48,519 --> 00:00:53,415
And almost all of them give you the same kind of answer: a label and a confidence number.

13
00:00:53,695 --> 00:00:58,111
"Banana, ninety-five percent." That number tells you nothing about why.

14
00:00:58,391 --> 00:01:00,263
Why does that matter?

15
00:01:00,543 --> 00:01:03,111
Because models can be right for the wrong reason.

16
00:01:03,391 --> 00:01:08,767
A famous example is a classifier that learned to spot a husky by the snow in the background.

17
00:01:09,047 --> 00:01:12,647
It looks accurate right up until you show it a husky on grass.

18
00:01:12,927 --> 00:01:16,239
So before I trust a model, I want to see its evidence.

19
00:01:16,519 --> 00:01:19,159
The technique I'm using is called Grad-CAM.

20
00:01:19,439 --> 00:01:25,703
It takes the model's own gradients, the signal it uses to learn, and turns them into a heat map.

21
00:01:25,983 --> 00:01:29,055
Red means "these pixels pushed the decision hard".

22
00:01:29,335 --> 00:01:31,207
Blue means "barely mattered".

23
00:01:31,487 --> 00:01:36,767
It does not need to retrain anything, and it works on almost any convolutional network.

24
00:01:37,567 --> 00:01:39,439
Here's the whole idea.

25
00:01:39,719 --> 00:01:44,663
A convolutional network, a CNN, slides small filters over the picture.

26
00:01:44,943 --> 00:01:46,815
Early layers find edges.

27
00:01:47,095 --> 00:01:51,247
Later layers find textures, then parts, then objects.

28
00:01:51,527 --> 00:02:01,079
By the last convolutional layer you have a stack of feature maps, each one answering a question like "is there something curved and yellow here?" at every location.

29
00:02:01,359 --> 00:02:06,903
Then the network collapses those maps into a score for each of its one thousand classes.

30
00:02:07,183 --> 00:02:14,791
The question Grad-CAM asks is simple: for the class I care about, say banana, how much does each feature map contribute?

31
00:02:15,071 --> 00:02:16,943
We find out with the gradient.

32
00:02:17,222 --> 00:02:23,726
Take the banana score, ask how it changes if each feature map changes, and average that over the whole map.

33
00:02:24,006 --> 00:02:25,878
That average is a weight.

34
00:02:26,158 --> 00:02:34,486
Multiply each feature map by its weight, add them up, throw away the negative bits, and stretch the result back to the size of the photo.

35
00:02:34,766 --> 00:02:36,638
That is your heat map.

36
00:02:36,918 --> 00:02:40,854
The model here is MobileNetV2, trained on ImageNet.

37
00:02:41,134 --> 00:02:49,750
It has about three and a half million parameters, small enough to run on my laptop CPU with no graphics card and no API calls at all.

38
00:02:50,550 --> 00:02:53,358
The app is one Python file with Streamlit.

39
00:02:53,638 --> 00:03:01,366
Loading the model is two lines: I ask torchvision for MobileNetV2 with the ImageNet weights, and put it in eval mode.

40
00:03:01,646 --> 00:03:04,430
Streamlit caches it so it loads once.

41
00:03:04,710 --> 00:03:06,582
Now the heart of it.

42
00:03:06,862 --> 00:03:08,734
Here is the real function.

43
00:03:09,014 --> 00:03:10,886
Let me walk through it.

44
00:03:11,166 --> 00:03:14,262
First, a forward hook on model.features.

45
00:03:14,542 --> 00:03:21,526
That's a little spy that grabs the output of the last convolutional block, the feature maps, while the image goes through.

46
00:03:21,806 --> 00:03:26,270
Then I run the model and take the softmax, which gives the probabilities.

47
00:03:26,550 --> 00:03:30,078
If you didn't tell me which class to explain, I use the winner.

48
00:03:30,358 --> 00:03:33,166
The key line is torch.autograd.grad.

49
00:03:33,446 --> 00:03:38,318
It asks: how does the score for my class change with respect to those feature maps?

50
00:03:38,598 --> 00:03:40,470
That is the gradient.

51
00:03:40,750 --> 00:03:45,862
Next, grads.mean over the height and width: one weight per feature map.

52
00:03:46,142 --> 00:03:53,894
Multiply the weights into the maps, sum, and apply ReLU, because I only care about evidence in favour of the class.

53
00:03:54,174 --> 00:03:59,262
Interpolate up to two hundred and twenty-four pixels, and normalise to zero-to-one.

54
00:03:59,542 --> 00:04:01,414
And that's it.

55
00:04:01,694 --> 00:04:03,566
Roughly twenty lines.

56
00:04:03,846 --> 00:04:05,862
Notice the cls argument.

57
00:04:06,142 --> 00:04:12,454
It lets me ask "show me where you looked for the second-best guess", which turns out to be the most fun part of the demo.

58
00:04:12,734 --> 00:04:24,038
The rest of the app is small: a function that blends red-to-blue colour into the photo with a strength slider, a top-five list, and an input chooser for samples, uploads or the webcam.

59
00:04:24,838 --> 00:04:26,710
Let's run it.

60
00:04:26,990 --> 00:04:28,862
Sample: banana.

61
00:04:29,142 --> 00:04:36,366
The left image is what the network really sees: a two-hundred-twenty-four pixel centre crop, because that's what it was trained on.

62
00:04:36,646 --> 00:04:39,262
In the middle, the Grad-CAM overlay.

63
00:04:39,542 --> 00:04:43,694
Banana, ninety-five percent, and the red sits right on the fruit.

64
00:04:43,974 --> 00:04:45,846
That's what I hoped for.

65
00:04:46,126 --> 00:04:50,062
I'll drag the heatmap strength slider to make the overlay stronger.

66
00:04:50,342 --> 00:04:52,598
Same evidence, easier to see.

67
00:04:52,878 --> 00:04:54,750
Now the cat.

68
00:04:55,030 --> 00:05:00,094
The top guess is tiger cat at twenty-two percent, and the next is tabby at thirteen.

69
00:05:00,374 --> 00:05:05,606
Not very confident, because ImageNet has several kinds of cat and they overlap.

70
00:05:05,886 --> 00:05:09,726
Watch what happens when I pick the second guess in "Explain which guess?".

71
00:05:10,006 --> 00:05:11,878
The heat moves.

72
00:05:12,158 --> 00:05:15,878
For tabby, it highlights different pixels than for tiger cat.

73
00:05:16,158 --> 00:05:19,038
Two answers, two different sets of evidence.

74
00:05:19,318 --> 00:05:21,190
Next, the dog.

75
00:05:21,470 --> 00:05:23,798
Labrador, around twenty percent.

76
00:05:24,078 --> 00:05:26,430
Again, much lower than the banana.

77
00:05:26,710 --> 00:05:29,518
I'll pick the runner-up and again the map changes.

78
00:05:29,798 --> 00:05:31,670
And the pizza.

79
00:05:31,950 --> 00:05:35,718
Pizza gets nineteen percent, and a frying pan gets eighteen.

80
00:05:35,998 --> 00:05:37,870
That is basically a coin flip.

81
00:05:38,150 --> 00:05:43,742
If I click on the frying pan, the heat map shows which pixels support that wrong-but-close answer.

82
00:05:44,022 --> 00:05:51,750
In this recording the heat spreads over the round pie, strongest toward the lower left, rather than sitting on one object.

83
00:05:52,030 --> 00:05:56,686
The point: a one-point gap means the model genuinely can't separate them.

84
00:05:56,966 --> 00:06:00,278
You can also upload your own photo, or use the webcam.

85
00:06:00,558 --> 00:06:02,814
I'd encourage you to try it with something weird.

86
00:06:03,614 --> 00:06:05,486
Three things surprised me.

87
00:06:05,766 --> 00:06:09,246
One: confidence is low in a lot of normal cases.

88
00:06:09,526 --> 00:06:12,166
Only the banana was above ninety percent.

89
00:06:12,446 --> 00:06:17,174
The cat, the Labrador and the pizza all came out under twenty-three percent.

90
00:06:17,454 --> 00:06:23,982
Because ImageNet splits the world into very fine categories, probability gets shared among neighbours.

91
00:06:24,262 --> 00:06:29,038
Low confidence does not mean wrong; it often means "several right answers".

92
00:06:29,318 --> 00:06:32,006
Two: the runner-up has its own evidence.

93
00:06:32,286 --> 00:06:35,718
The heat map for the second guess lives in different pixels.

94
00:06:35,998 --> 00:06:38,230
A single number hides that completely.

95
00:06:38,510 --> 00:06:40,622
Three: how little code it took.

96
00:06:40,902 --> 00:06:44,190
The whole explanation technique is about twenty lines.

97
00:06:44,470 --> 00:06:49,678
And a bonus I should be honest about: a heat map looks convincing, and that is a risk.

98
00:06:49,958 --> 00:06:57,134
It looks like an explanation, but it's a coarse picture, because the last block's feature map is small and then stretched up.

99
00:06:57,934 --> 00:06:59,854
Let me be straight about the limits.

100
00:07:00,134 --> 00:07:05,630
Grad-CAM is low resolution; it tells you roughly where, not exactly which pixel.

101
00:07:05,910 --> 00:07:10,014
It explains this model's gradients, not the model's "thoughts".

102
00:07:10,294 --> 00:07:13,846
The network only knows one thousand ImageNet classes.

103
00:07:14,126 --> 00:07:22,982
Show it something else, a drawing, a person, a spoon in a weird light, and it will still pick one of the thousand and sometimes sound sure about it.

104
00:07:23,262 --> 00:07:27,438
The app says that in a caption, and I think it's the most important line in it.

105
00:07:27,718 --> 00:07:33,214
Also, I only tried a handful of sample photos, so don't read my percentages as a benchmark.

106
00:07:33,494 --> 00:07:36,398
They are what this model produced on these four images.

107
00:07:36,678 --> 00:07:39,222
And yes, I wrote this with Claude Code.

108
00:07:39,502 --> 00:07:44,662
I read and ran the code, but I would call myself the director, not the sole author.

109
00:07:45,462 --> 00:07:47,334
What next?

110
00:07:47,614 --> 00:08:02,494
Ideas on my list: an attention visualiser for the tiny GPT I built earlier, so we can do the same X-ray for language; swapping in a bigger network and comparing heat maps; and a bias demo showing the husky-on-snow failure on purpose.

111
00:08:02,774 --> 00:08:08,822
If you enjoyed this, I built sixteen AI and ML projects like it and the code is in the description.

112
00:08:09,102 --> 00:08:12,702
Subscribe, and tell me in the comments which photo I should feed it.

113
00:08:12,982 --> 00:08:14,854
Thanks for watching.
